The task was already finished before the interruption. Commit 46cc56c3 holds the whole change, and nothing uncommitted was lost.

What a user gets: a multi-line list under [permissions] or [directories] now builds its fence. Before, this standard TOML shape:

```toml
[permissions]
deny = [
  "rm *",
]
```

printed "could not read … This rule is NOT in force" and no deny rule existed. The interrupted agent's own check on the built binary in a temp repo (I didn't re-run it) found that yoyo permissions now lists ✗ rm *, and doctor is silent. Dotted keys, inline tables, comments inside or after the array, and [ permissions ] with spaces all read the same.

Behaviour changes, named in the commit:
• Bad values now warn. A value that isn't a list (deny = "rm *") or entries that aren't strings print a warning that names the key and says those rules are not in force. The string entries of a mixed list still apply, so one bad entry doesn't drop the whole deny list. Before, a string value was ignored without a word, and a bare 1 became the rule "1".
• `/cd` into a non-TOML `.yoyo.toml` now reports the file as unreadable, with none of it applied. Before, it showed up as an empty deny list, which quietly let everything through. This put a fifth file in the change (cd_config_note.rs), outside the four the task listed.

